$###########################################################################

$ THERMODYNAMIC PARAMETERS: H_BCC  

$###########################################################################

$

 TYPE_DEFINITION S GES A_P_D H_BCC MAGNETIC  -1.0    0.4 !

 PHASE H_BCC  %S  2 1   1 ! 

$bcc_a2 Fe with H-solubility using alternative sublattice description 

$with less interstitial sites, e.g. Ref 223 

$>> 1 !

    CONSTITUENT H_BCC  : FE% : H,VA% :  !

$-------------------------------------------------------------------

$ BCC_A2 endmember compounds with VA

$-------------------------------------------------------------------

PARAMETER G(H_BCC,FE:VA;


PARAMETER G(H_BCC,FE:H;

  -0.0894166963*T**2+GHSERFE#+0.5*GHSERHH#;

  +80393.95+16.72144*T+GHSERFE#+0.5*GHSERHH#;


PARAMETER TC(H_BCC,FE:VA;


PARAMETER BMAGN(H_BCC,FE:VA;




$###########################################################################

$                   THERMODYNAMIC PARAMETERS:   B C C _ D I S L

$###########################################################################

$

$ Implemented koze April 2010



PHASE BCC_DISL  %&  2  1   3 ! 

$This phase is used to model Cottrell atmosphere formation; 

$you should use it with these data only for interstitial segregation, 

$since uncontrolled behaviour if substitutional also segregate. 

$Interactions only defined up to a temperature of 1000�C 

$>> 2 !

    CONSTITUENT BCC_DISL  : AL,CO,CR,CU,FE%,MN,MO,NB,NI,S,SI,TI,V,W : 

                          B,C,N,VA% :  !

$



PARAMETER G(BCC_DISL,*:C,VA;


PARAMETER G(BCC_DISL,*:N,VA;




PARAMETER G(BCC_DISL,FE:C,VA;


PARAMETER G(BCC_DISL,FE:N,VA;


$

$###########################################################################

$                   THERMODYNAMIC PARAMETERS: HCP_A3 

$###########################################################################

$

 TYPE_DEFINITION ) GES A_P_D HCP_A3 MAGNETIC  -3.0    0.28 !

 PHASE HCP_A3  %)  2 1   0.5 ! 

$Hexagonal close-packed. M2C, denoted "hcp#01" in the MatCalc databases, 

$with C on interstitial sites and Mo as mayor constituent. 

$Cr and V can dissolve with significant quantities in M2C. M2C carbide 

$and M2N nitride composition sets can be

$defined by the user (syntax see top of database). 

$M2C is an important precipitate phase, e.g. in complex tool steels, etc. 

$M2C is commonly precipitating on dislocations 

$and/or martensite lath boundaries. 

$>> 3 !

    CONSTITUENT HCP_A3  : AL,CO%,CR,CU,FE,HF,MN,MO,NB,NI,PD,SI,TI%,V,W,Y : 

                        B,C,N,O,VA% :  !

